|~ What is declared under `::string`: the refusal of its
   constructor, and the verbs of strings: the comparisons, which compare
   two strings by their code units in the one order, the verbs that read
   a string, `count`, `take` and `drop` by its characters, and
   `prepend` and `append`, under the contracts of their names on
   `::any`. ~|

:string/PayloadNotStringError
  |~~ `::string` reads its payload as the string it is; a value of
      another kind becomes text by `print`.
      ~(
        ::string(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/PayloadNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:string/ParseJsonNumberNotFiniteError
  |~~ JSON text may write a number past the range of the doubles a
      number of qlang is, `1e400`, which no number of the language
      holds, so reading it is refused rather than turned into an
      infinity. It is met where JSON text is read, by `parseJson` and
      by the command line reading its input, and never in a literal of
      qlang, which the parser refuses itself.

      ~("1e400" | parseJson !| type | eq ::string/ParseJsonNumberNotFiniteError)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:string/gt
  |~~ Whether the subject follows the string the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("b" | gt "a" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/gt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ::holds~(isA ::string) ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/gte
  |~~ Whether the subject follows or equals the string the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("b" | gte "b" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/gte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ::holds~(isA ::string) ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lt
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes the string the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("a" | lt "b" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ::holds~(isA ::string) ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lte
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes or equals the string the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("a" | lte "a" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ::holds~(isA ::string) ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/count
  |~~ The number of characters of the string, a letter of any script
      one, the length every other language gives a string. ::holds~(isA ::number)

      ~("abc" | count | eq 3)
      ~("привет" | count | eq 6)
      ~("" | count | eq 0)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/take
  |~~ The first count characters of the string. ::keeps~()

      ~("db_host" | take 2 | eq "db")
      ~("ab" | take 5 | eq "ab")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/take/count
  |~~ How many. ::holds~(isA ::number) ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/drop
  |~~ The string without its first count characters, the rest after a
      prefix of known length. ::keeps~()

      ~("db_host" | drop 3 | eq "host")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ::holds~(isA ::number) ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/split
  |~~ The substrings between the occurrences of the separator, as a
      vector. Inverse of `join`. ::holds~(isA ::vec)

      ~("a,b,c" | split "," | eq ["a" "b" "c"])
      ~("line1\nline2" | split "\n" | eq ["line1" "line2"])

      The subject of `split` is the string it cuts; a vector of strings
      is cut element by element, `* split ","`.
      ~(
        42 | split ","
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/split/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/split/separator
  |~~ The separator the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      The separator of `split` is a string, `split ","`.
      ~(
        "abc" | split 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/split/separator/HoldsNotMetError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lines
  |~~ The lines of a text, as a vector of strings. A `\n` ends a line
      and a `\r` before it belongs to the ending; a newline at the end
      closes the last line rather than opening an empty one, so a text
      reads alike with and without it, and the empty text has no lines.
      The text is then worked on as the vector it has become, and
      `join "\n"` writes it back. ::holds~(isA ::vec)

      ~("north\nsouth" | lines | eq ["north" "south"])
      ~("one\r\ntwo\n" | lines | eq ["one" "two"])
      ~("" | lines | eq [])

      `lines` breaks a string, the text the command line reads with
      `--raw`; a vector of texts is broken element by element, `* lines`.
      ~(
        42 | lines
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/lines/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lower
  |~~ The string with every letter in lower case, so two strings that
      differ in case alone compare equal once lowered. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      ~("Ann@Example.COM" | lower | eq "ann@example.com")
      ~(["Ann" "ANN"] * lower | distinct | count | eq 1)

      `lower` works on a string; a vector of strings takes it element by
      element, `* lower`, and a field is read first, `/email | lower`.
      ~(
        42 | lower
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/lower/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/upper
  |~~ The string with every letter in upper case. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      ~("ok, go" | upper | eq "OK, GO")

      `upper` works on a string; a vector of strings takes it element by
      element, `* upper`.
      ~(
        42 | upper
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/upper/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/trim
  |~~ The string without the whitespace at its two ends, spaces, tabs
      and line breaks, so a field cut out of a line compares as the text
      it holds. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      ~("  db_host 
" | trim | eq "db_host")
      ~("a, b ,c" | split "," * trim | eq ["a" "b" "c"])

      `trim` works on a string; a vector of strings takes it element by
      element, `* trim`.
      ~(
        42 | trim
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/trim/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/contains
  |~~ Whether the string holds the needle as a substring; the empty
      needle is always held. Case-sensitive. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("hello world" | contains "world" | eq true)
      ~("hello" | contains "xyz" | eq false)

      The subject of `::string/contains` is the string searched, where the
      address calls it; the name `contains` reaches a vector and a set by
      their elements.
      ~(
        42 | string/contains "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/contains/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/contains/needle
  |~~ The text looked for. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      The needle of `contains` is a string, `contains "x"`; a keyword
      gives its name by `keyword`.
      ~(
        "hi" | contains 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/contains/needle/HoldsNotMetError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/startsWith
  |~~ Whether the string begins with the prefix; the empty prefix
      always begins it. Case-sensitive. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("hello world" | startsWith "hello" | eq true)
      ~("hello" | startsWith "world" | eq false)

      The subject of `startsWith` is the string whose beginning is read;
      a field is read first, `/name | startsWith "a"`.
      ~(
        42 | startsWith "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/startsWith/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/startsWith/prefix
  |~~ The prefix the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      The prefix of `startsWith` is a string, `startsWith "he"`.
      ~(
        "hi" | startsWith 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/startsWith/prefix/HoldsNotMetError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/endsWith
  |~~ Whether the string ends with the suffix; the empty suffix always
      ends it. Case-sensitive. ::holds~(isA ::boolean)

      ~("hello world" | endsWith "world" | eq true)
      ~("hello" | endsWith "xyz" | eq false)

      The subject of `endsWith` is the string whose end is read; a field
      is read first, `/file | endsWith ".json"`.
      ~(
        42 | endsWith "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/endsWith/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/endsWith/suffix
  |~~ The suffix the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      The suffix of `endsWith` is a string, `endsWith ".json"`.
      ~(
        "hi" | endsWith 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/endsWith/suffix/HoldsNotMetError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/prepend
  |~~ The string with the prefix in front; a tag over the string comes
      back on the answer. ::keeps~()

      ~("world" | prepend "hello " | eq "hello world")
      ~(::Box("b") | prepend "a" | eq ::Box("ab"))
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/prepend/prefix
  |~~ The prefix the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      The prefix a string takes is a string; a value of another kind
      becomes one by `print`, `prepend (n | print)`.
      ~(
        "x" | prepend 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/prepend/prefix/HoldsNotMetError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/append
  |~~ The string with the suffix after it; a tag over the string comes
      back on the answer. ::keeps~()

      ~("hello" | append " world" | eq "hello world")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/append/suffix
  |~~ The suffix the call gives. ::holds~(isA ::string)

      The suffix a string takes is a string; a value of another kind
      becomes one by `print`, `append (n | print)`.
      ~(
        "x" | append 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/append/suffix/HoldsNotMetError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/parseJson
  |~~ The qlang value a string of plain JSON spells, the reverse of
      `json`: the keys of an object become keywords, an array a vector,
      and scalars pass through. Lossy for the shapes of qlang alone: a
      set arriving from JSON reads as a vector, a keyword as a value as
      a string, and errors have no plain form. It reads input from any
      producer that speaks JSON.

      ~("42" | parseJson | eq 42)
      ~("\"hello\"" | parseJson | eq "hello")
      ~("[1,2,3]" | parseJson | eq [1 2 3])
      ~("{\"name\":\"alice\"}" | parseJson | eq {:name "alice"})

      `parseJson` reads a string of JSON text; a value becomes such text
      by `json`.

      ~(
        42 | parseJson
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::string/parseJson/SubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/ParseJsonTextNotJsonError
  |~~ `parseJson` could not parse the subject as JSON. The message
      of the host's parser rides on `:message`.

      ~("{not json" | parseJson !| type | eq ::string/ParseJsonTextNotJsonError)
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:string/keyword
  |~~ The keyword the string names, bridging the world of strings, JSON
      data and text built from parts, with the world of keywords, the
      keys of maps and the discriminators of enums. ::holds~(isA ::keyword)

      ~("foo" | keyword | eq :foo)
      ~("qlang/error" | keyword | eq :qlang/error)
      ~("bar" | keyword | keyword | eq "bar")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/parse
  |~~ The quote of the steps the source text spells, the reverse of
      `parse` on a quote. Pair it with `apply /` to evaluate source
      text without leaving qlang. A source the parser refuses answers a
      `::quote/ParseError` on the fail-track, which `!|` reads like every
      other error.

      ~("1 | add 1" | parse | count | eq 2)
      ~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}
